{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q3BeBJPoKsd6kDRAp2kAot","type":"tool_result","content":"okens. 0.5–1.5B on an idle slice: 5–15 min.\n- `AMead10/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-AWQ` downloads in <1 min and runs in a ~4 GB slice up to ~9K context. Qwen3-0.6B (non-thinking) answers \"number or name\" questions with a number 95% of the time.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (cot_verbosity_tax workspace, 6+ concurrent neurico sessions): GPUs were ~fully oversubscribed; launches failed ~80% from free-memory races. Lessons:\n- Long multi-round generation (budget forcing, 12 rounds × up to 2k tokens) on a 100%-util shared GPU is very slow: 7B-AWQ forced arm took ~4 h incl. a ~60 min final answer pass (small KV → ~15 concurrent long contexts). Budget for it, or cut rounds/items for ≥7B.\n- My own jobs on the same GPU slow each other badly — don't stack 7B+14B+alt runs on one GPU.\n- Two of my queues polling independently launched on the same GPU in the same second and both failed: use ONE queue. A queue restart loses track of its running children → mark them (EXTERNAL file) or it relaunches duplicates. Stop leftover launch.sh wrappers at session end (they relaunch after you kill the child).\n- Qwen3-4B-AWQ (download ~2.5 GB) works as a 4.4 GB-slice LLM judge: ~1k rubric labels in ~4 min, 0 parse failures, but applies categories loosely — audit samples.\n- pandas 3: groupby().apply drops grouping columns — restore them by index join.\n- Background Bash with run_in_background still caps at 10 min when a timeout isn't given — set timeout explicitly for long waits.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (positional_retrieval_decay workspace): GPUs saturated again by ~6 parallel neurico sessions, each running retry loops that race for the same freed slices. What worked:\n- **Retry engine creation in-process** (poll nvidia-smi, size budget = min(max, free−0.35), retry LLM() after failures) instead of relaunching; build prompts AFTER engine init to shrink the race window.\n- **Work-stealing pool** (src/worker_pool.py): one thread per GPU launches the highest-priority job whose min budget fits; per-tag flock files prevent duplicates; exit code 75 = \"gave up waiting\" (not a failure). Chunked generation with append-only .partial files (whole samples) made kills/resumes safe and let analysis use partial runs.\n- Duplicate-run gotcha: a \"move finished .jsonl back to .partial\" extension script raced a queue that retried the same tag → two writers on one file. Use the lock in every code path.\n- 7–8B AWQ on a GPU shared by ~7 processes: 12–21 min per 720 prompts of avg 3K tokens; ≤3B models: 0.5–3 min. Budget large models at N/3.\n- WikiText-103 test+val gives only ~49 passages of ≥7.5K words (~10K tokens) → a \"16K\" haystack silently capped at ~10K; check ctx_tokens per length.\n- Single-needle retrieval is at ceiling for Qwen2.5 ≥1.5B / Llama-8B / Qwen3-4B up to 10K; near-miss distractors (same name, different type) are the discriminating condition.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (prompt_surface_sensitivity workspace, 10 models × 24k prompts, vLLM 0.32):\n- Shared-GPU startup races were lost ~90% of the time (other sessions fill a freed slice during vLLM's ~20 s startup). What fixed it: TP=1 → `VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0` (in-process engine) + grab memory with a `torch.empty(budget)` tensor as soon as free, and monkeypatch `vllm.v1.worker.gpu_worker.MemorySnapshot` to free the tensor + `empty_cache()` just before vLLM measures. TP=2 → per-GPU holder subprocesses + `VLLM_WORKER_MULTIPROC_METHOD=fork` so forked workers inherit the patch and SIGKILL their GPU's holder. TP workers create CUDA ctx+NCCL (~0.8 GB) *before* the snapshot, so a 5.0 GB slice can't host TP; need ≥5.6 GB/GPU. Holders must exit when their parent dies (poll getppid), else orphans hog memory. Code: that workspace's src/generate.py, src/gpu_queue.py.\n- Llama-3.x and Gemma-2 chat templates `| trim` message content (trailing whitespace is a no-op); Qwen2.5 keeps it — useful as a free placebo.\n- vLLM greedy decoding is not batch-invariant: byte-identical prompts in different batch positions gave identical long (≤1024-tok) outputs only 23–76% of the time, moving IFEval accuracy by up to ~3 pp; short (≤10-tok) outputs ~99.7% identical.\n- Two GPU queues launching TP jobs onto the same GPU pair race each other — run one queue.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-22 (sampling_temp_misinformation workspace, 7 models × 9 temps × n=10, vLLM 0.32):\n- GPU2 was fully free at 11:20 and ~37 GB of it was taken within 5 min — launch immediately.\n- In-process hold (VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0 + torch.empty placeholder + MemorySnapshot subclass patched in both\n  `vllm.utils.mem_utils` and `vllm.v1.worker.gpu_worker`) started 3B/7B-AWQ jobs on the first try. Re-acquire the hold\n  before each retry, or retries run without it and fail. Pick the GPU (max free) before importing torch, then pin to it.\n- Qwen2.5-3B bf16 at an 8.5 GB budget (non-eager, 4096 batched tokens) → \"No available memory for the cache blocks\".\n  3B-AWQ in 4.4 GB works.\n- SamplingParams(n=10, seed=s) seeds the samples consecutively → requests with seeds s and s+1 share 9/10 streams\n  (common random numbers across conditions). Space per-request seeds ≥ n apart if independence is wanted.\n- GSM8K 512-token CoT × 81 samples × 400 items on a 100%-util shared GPU: 1.5–3 h per ≤3B model; the QA part takes minutes.\n- Paired Wilcoxon on fractional, zero-heavy item-level diffs (sample-mean minus 0/1) contradicts the bootstrap CIs;\n  use a sign-flip permutation test of the mean.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-22 (embedding_drift_finetuning workspace, LoRA fine-tuning 160M–7B on shared GPUs):\n- A fully free GPU (48 GB) at session start was reservable with a tiny holder (`torch.empty(36GB)` + exit on a RELEASE file) while code was written; have the training script touch RELEASE after loading weights to CPU, just before `.cuda()`. But whatever you don't hold gets taken within minutes, and the 7B LoRA run (17 GB) still OOM'd mid-run when co-tenants grew — budget 7B training as infeasible unless a GPU is dedicated. On a GPU at 100% util shared with another user, 7B LoRA bs4×512 ran ~7 s/step; 3B 4.6 s/step; 1.5B 2.9 s/step; checkpoint evals (1.6k probes + 1,070 MCQ items) 4–8.5 min. 0.5B LoRA fits a 5 GB slice (2.4 GB peak, 20 min/3k steps); 1.5B peaks 4.5 GB.\n- \"Idle\" 5–6 GB slices at 0% util are transient: other users' 41–43 GB jobs regrow and OOM you minutes later.\n- Worker queue bug: a worker that retries the first unclaimed job starves smaller jobs if that job never fits → per-job memory need + skip; and when killing a worker loop, its job lock dir is orphaned (rmdir it). Writing reps before accuracy evals means a mid-eval kill leaves a checkpoint with reps but no metrics — filter to steps present in metrics.jsonl.\n- Science gotcha: always log held-out in-domain loss — Pythia-160M LoRA (r64, α128, lr 2e-4) diverged in every domain, and Qwen2.5 base on codeparrot Python showed no in-domain gain; \"drift\" results otherwise get misread.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-23 (repetition_penalty_sideeffects workspace): GPU2 showed 48 GB free at 12:21 but was taken before vLLM was even installed (~10 min) — the uv install of vllm took >10 min (run it in background immediately). 7B-AWQ at 6.3–8.4 GB never started all session (all GPUs ≤1.5 GB free from 12:40); ≤3B AWQ at 4.2 GB ran fine. The user's `vllm serve Qwen3-8B` on :8011 had only ~2% KV usage at 24 concurrent requests — use 48–64 concurrency (~6 gen/s for 300-token outputs; LLM-judge ~75–100 calls/min). It accepts `repetition_penalty` and `chat_template_kwargs: {enable_thinking: false}` in /v1/chat/completions. HF `model.generate` with a custom LogitsProcessor (1–1.5B bf16, bs 50, 320 new tokens) took 5–45 s/batch on a shared slice — fine for ablations vLLM can't express.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-23 (batch_size_inference_quality workspace, 10 configs × 1,700 prompts × 7 batch conditions, vLLM 0.32):\n- GPU2 showed 48 GB free at 12:22 but a sibling session (3 neurico sessions start together at 12:00) took 40 GB within ~1 min — the free GPU at session start is a race you'll usually lose; plan for ~5 GB slices.\n- Killing a job to restart it with different settings loses its slice within seconds (other sessions' wait-loops grab it) — prefer env/file-driven behaviour changes decided at launch; unpinned (no CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES) relaunch with a smaller budget got a slice again.\n- Static-chunk batching in one engine (`llm.generate(chunk_of_B)`) is a clean batch-size manipulation. B=1 eager on a shared GPU: ~50–60 min (0.5–1.5B) for 1,700 prompts incl. 700 × ≤512-token CoT; 3B bf16 B=4 ~2 h → budget B=1 on subsets for ≥3B.\n- Result worth reusing: vLLM bf16 greedy is deterministic for an identical schedule (0 diffs) but batch-dependent: B=1 vs B≥4 changes 47–76% of CoT outputs; fp16 ~5× and fp32 ~12× fewer; divergences start at bf16 top-2 ties (logprob margin 0 or 0.125).\n- Analysis gotcha: iterating `set(a) & set(b)` of string ids gives PYTHONHASHSEED-dependent order → bootstrap CIs not reproducible; sort first.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-23 (system_prompt_saturation workspace, 9 models × 3.6k prompts up to 8k tokens, vLLM 0.32):\n- GPU2 fully free at 12:20; grabbed it with a chunked holder (1 GB tensors, target size read from a control file) — but\n  the 9 GB freed between two of my jobs was taken by other sessions within ~1 min, and later jobs got only 5–13 GB.\n- vLLM memory profiling charges other tenants' growth during startup to *your* \"non-torch\" usage (8B AWQ at 13 GB got\n  1.1 GB KV → startup ValueError). Fix: `LLM(kv_cache_memory_bytes=...)` skips profiling entirely; size the hold as\n  weights + KV + ~1.5 GB.\n- Holder handoff bugs: compute the holder's *actual* size from `nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps` for its PID, not the\n  target file; and on a 100%-full GPU, CUDA context creation raises `torch.AcceleratorError` (a RuntimeError), not\n  OutOfMemoryError — catch both and wait for the holder to free before touching CUDA.\n- Per-output-file `fcntl.flock` in generate.py (print \"LOCKED … 0 to do\" and exit 0) made several queues on different\n  GPUs safe against duplicate writers.\n- Timings on a GPU shared by ~8 processes: 8B AWQ with a 3.5 GB KV (28k tokens) needed 3 h for 3.6k prompts (1.2k of\n  them 1–8k tokens); 14B AWQ with 13.6k-token KV couldn't finish long prompts at all. Short-prompt experiments for\n  big models first, long-context ones only if KV ≥ ~20k tokens.\n- Greedy re-run of Qwen2.5-1.5B on a different GPU/KV size: 13% byte-identical texts but rule-check agreement 93.7%\n  and mean metric within 0.07 pp.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-26 (lora_rank_blind_spots workspace, ~170 LoRA train+eval jobs of Qwen2.5-0.5B/1.5B, HF+peft, no vLLM):\n- GPUs had 0.5–6 GB free at 10:40, then ~25–43 GB freed on 3 GPUs at ~11:24 for ~10 min. A queue that only launches one job per GPU every 75 s wastes such a window; LAUNCH_GAP≈20–30 s filled it.\n- Without a concurrency cap my queue launched 52 jobs → load avg 60 on 32 cores, each job 5× slower. Cap running jobs (~15–20) and set OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 per job.\n- Mid-run OOMs from co-tenants: reserving memory at start (`torch.empty(RESERVE_GB)`, then del → it stays in PyTorch's cache) and saving the LoRA adapter right after training (so eval-only retries are possible) stopped most of the lost work. Real peak for a 0.5B LoRA job was 2.3–4.8 GB, more than estimated. HF generate prefill on 32 prompts × ~50 tokens still allocates ~0.5 GB of full-vocab logits.\n- After gradient_checkpointing_disable(), pass use_cache=True to generate explicitly.\n- Timings under contention: 0.5B LoRA, 250 steps, bs16 → 1.5 min (short classification inputs, idle GPU) up to 25–30 min (GSM8K/code, ~20 co-running jobs).\n\nUpdate 2026-10-26 (greedy_vs_nucleus_crossover workspace, 6 models × 6 tasks × 6 decoding conds, ~144k gens):\n- GPUs again ~4 GB free per GPU at 0% util. Qwen2.5-{0.5,1.5,3}B-Instruct-AWQ all ran in ≤3.7 GB slices (weights + kv 0.6–0.8 GB + ~1.0 GB overhead, `kv_cache_memory_bytes`, enforce_eager) and started first try; 7B-AWQ (7.4 GB) got a slice after ~35 min of waiting.\n- The user's `vllm serve Qwen3-8B` on :8011 was idle again: 8.1k chat requests (n=3, up to 512 tok) in ~25 min at 48 threads; single-token logprob judge (max_tokens=1, top_logprobs=20, probability-weighted 1–9) did 7.2k ratings in ~5–7 min. Digit mass ≈1.0.\n- Pooled-sample entropy conflates model errors with answer multiplicity (small models' wrong TriviaQA answers look \"high entropy\"); restrict to valid outputs for a gold-entropy proxy.\n- CPU sentence-embedding of ~40k texts under load avg ~50 took ~15 min; cache embeddings to disk for re-runs.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-26 (kv_cache_compression_artifacts workspace, HF-transformers 5.20 custom-attention work): no API key; Qwen3-8B server on :8011 again available as judge. GPUs at ~4 GB free at 10:40, then 0/1/2 freed to ~40 GB at ~11:24 for ~5 min (grab immediately; 7B bf16 at 16 GB later lost its slot during a worker hand-off — never kill a placed worker to \"restart with other args\"). Lessons:\n- Cache-policy experiments without vLLM: register a custom fn via `transformers.AttentionInterface`, run `model.model(..., use_cache=False, position_ids=...)` and keep your own KV store inside the fn (no mask needed: custom names skip HF mask creation). ~1 s/item for 1.5B at 6k ctx, 128-token chunks.\n- place_model pattern: load to CPU, poll nvidia-smi, `.cuda()` + warm-up forward + reserve; on failure `os._exit(75)` and relaunch from a bash loop (a failed in-process attempt leaves a 0.4 GB CUDA ctx pinned on that GPU).\n- Teacher-forced log_softmax over 256×152k vocab OOM'd a 4 GB slice mid-job → job lock orphaned with 4/150 rows. After a grid, check every output file's row count.\n- Qwen2.5 naive per-token 4-bit KV quant is catastrophic (key outlier channels); KIVI per-channel keys fine. Llama-3.2 fine either way.\n- LLM-judge for contradictions: free-form \"list contradictions\" with Qwen3-8B over-reports; slot extraction + deterministic comparison worked.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-27 (tokenizer_merge_order workspace): **numpy ≥2 allocations >4 MB hang indefinitely** (100% CPU, e.g. `np.ones(5M uint16)`, `arr.astype(int64)`) because numpy madvises hugepages and THP defrag=madvise stalls on this host. Fix: `export NUMPY_MADVISE_HUGEPAGE=0` for every python process (bytearray/torch allocs were fine). py-spy can't attach (no ptrace); use `python -X faulthandler` + `faulthandler.dump_traceback_later(40)`. GPUs 0–2 showed 48 GB free at 11:03 and were ~3 GB free by 11:08; GPU3 sat at 6.1 GB free / 0% util. Tiny-LM pretraining (23M params, ctx 512, bf16) ≈ 300k tok/s on an idle slice, ~2.5 GB at micro-batch 16.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-27 (gradient_masking_safety workspace, 14 models 0.5–32B × 5.4k prompts, vLLM 0.32): 3 GPUs were fully free at 11:03 and taken within ~10 min; grabbing 40 GB immediately with a 1-GB-chunk holder paid off. What worked best:\n- ONE long-lived in-process worker (VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0) that runs all models sequentially and never returns memory to the driver: disable `torch.cuda.empty_cache`/`torch.accelerator.empty_cache`, drop placeholder chunks into PyTorch's cache at `MemorySnapshot.measure`, and add `memory_reserved()-memory_allocated()` to the snapshot's free_memory. Releasing to the driver at measure time lost ~9 GB to other tenants during weight loading (OOM). Queue entries `task=<fn>:<arg>` dispatched via importlib.reload(tasks) let code change without restarting (a restart = handoff race). Handoff to a new worker: TAKEOVER_PID tight-loop grab after the old pid exits kept 26/26 GB.\n- The :8011 Qwen3-8B server intermittently returned CORRUPTED logprobs (sampled token != top-logprob argmax, top-k mass ~1e-6); it looked like a judge \"safety reflex\" (refusals scored as compliance, 9/37 accuracy). Validate every logprob judgment (argmax==sampled token, mass>=0.5, retry, fall back to text). With validation, ~10–19 judgments/s.\n- Judge design that validated (37 hand labels): reply-only \"does the assistant decline?\" (34/37) + request+reply \"does the reply contain content that carries out the request?\" (35/37). cais/HarmBench-Mistral-7b-val-cls weights downloaded to the cache (~14 GB, 3 min).\n- Timings on the shared A6000 (26 GB slice): ≤1.5B 2–4 min, 3B ~18 min, Phi-3 35 min, 7–9B 10–22 min, 14B-AWQ 8 min, 32B-AWQ 40 min per ~4–5k prompts (≤256 tokens), varying with contention.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-29 (fewshot_example_contamination workspace, 8 models × 3,150 gens, vLLM 0.32): load avg 230–350; `ps aux`/`pgrep -af` hang on /proc of stuck processes — use `pgrep -x uv -u $USER` + /proc/<pid>/{stat,wchan,cwd} with `timeout`.\n- **uv install blocked for 10+ min**: 3 sibling sessions' `uv add` all waited on the shared ~/.cache/uv lock (futex) and copies cache(/ root FS)→/data. Fix: `UV_CACHE_DIR=$PWD/.uv_cache uv sync` (same FS → hardlinks; fresh download of vllm stack ~3 min, \"Installed 215 packages in 7.8s\"). A killed uv became a zombie whose stuck threads still held `.venv/.lock` (find holder via `grep :<inode> /proc/locks`) → `mv .venv .venv_stuck_old` and recreate.\n- No-dep server client: `/usr/bin/python3 -I` + urllib works against :8011 while the venv installs.\n- Lane-script bug: `grep -q \"0 trials to do\"` matches \"3150 trials to do\" → failed launches counted as done. Anchor on `^<tag>: 0 trials to do`.\n- GPU0 was fully free 13:15–13:40; 7B/8B/14B AWQ all started first try with `kv_cache_memory_bytes` + gpu_memory_utilization=(budget+0.2)/48. :8011 Qwen3-8B did 3,150 short gens in ~25 min when idle, but crawled (~20/min) once other users loaded it to 110 concurrent requests.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-29 (context_window_cliff workspace, long-context 1K–32K probes, vLLM 0.32): no API key again; `uv add vllm` took ~15 min (slow copy, disk 98%). :8011 Qwen3-8B server was shared with another session (~30–45 running reqs) yet did 1,548 prompts of avg 12K tokens in ~110 min at 16 threads. All my local jobs auto-landed on GPU0 (100% util) → 7B-AWQ ~3.9 prompts/min at 16K avg; moving 1.5B/3B AWQ to the *idle* GPU1 4.2 GB slice cut ETA ~8× (actual use ≈ weights+kv+0.5 GB, so OVERHEAD=0.5, MARGIN=0.25 fits). Shuffling prompt order before running makes any partial run a uniform subsample — kill slow jobs at the deadline without losing design balance. Science gotcha: Phi-3-mini-4k (kaitchup GPTQ) has sliding_window 2047 honoured by vLLM → retrieval of facts >2K tokens from the question ≈0% (looks like a \"length cliff\").\n\nUpdate 2026-10-29 (invisible_formatting_effects workspace, 11 models × 20k prompts, vLLM 0.32): `uv add vllm` took ~20 min (load avg 230–340, slow file copy) — start it first thing. GPU0 briefly had 18 GB free at 13:26 and then 20 GB at 14:49 — launching a worker immediately both times paid off. What worked:\n- **Worker hand-over without losing the slice** (to change code, queue order or registry): the new worker gets `TAKEOVER_PID=<old>`, finishes its imports, writes a ready file, busy-waits until /proc/<old> disappears, then creates its CUDA context and grabs 512 MB chunks. Kill the old PID in a *separate* call once the ready file exists. This kept 5/5 GB and 15/15 GB. It was used to move 14B code generation behind 7B/8B, triggered automatically by a log line (\"cls 14400/14400\").\n- Old workers re-read the shared queue file but keep their imported MODELS registry → adding new keys to that queue would KeyError them. Give new workers their own queue file.\n- The :8011 Qwen3-8B server was fast at first (~3.5k short chat requests in 90 s), then other users loaded it (48 running + 63 waiting), and the last ~2.5k 512-token code requests took ~2 h.\n- Re-running the same checkpoint in a separate engine (same prompts, same shuffle) reproduced 19,968/19,968 outputs byte-identically; a byte-identical prompt at a different batch position changed 2–21% of 512-token code outputs but ~0.02% of short labels.\n- Qwen2.5-14B-AWQ on a 15 GB slice of a 100%-util GPU: ~5 min per 1.5k short prompts, ~13 min per 1.5k 512-token code prompts.\n/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/system_prompt_interference_20261030_120001_ef56gh78\ntotal 89\ndrwxrws---   9 summereunann datausers   10 Oct 30 13:17 .\ndrwxrwsrwx 124 summereunann datausers  133 Oct 30 13:17 ..\ndrwxrws---   2 summereunann datausers    2 Oct 30 13:17 artifacts\ndrwxrws---   3 summereunann datausers    3 Oct 30 13:17 .claude\ndrwxrws---   3 summereunann datausers    3 Oct 30 13:17 .codex\ndrwxrws---   3 summereunann datausers    3 Oct 30 13:17 .gemini\n-rw-rw-rw-   1 summereunann datausers 3410 Jul 30 17:02 .gitignore\ndrwxrws---   2 summereunann datausers    6 Oct 30 13:17 logs\ndrwxrws---   2 summereunann datausers    3 Oct 30 13:17 .neurico\ndrwxrws---   2 summereunann datausers    2 Oct 30 13:17 results\nCLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH=<set>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17b87f01-dc5c-4ec6-a3b0-c76322b2a627","uuid":"53915eee-c2a4-4a70-b9de-1f6bd9e54aeb","timestamp":"2026-10-30T18:17:34.416Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"okens. 0.5–1.5B on an idle slice: 5–15 min.\n- `AMead10/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-AWQ` downloads in <1 min and runs in a ~4 GB slice up to ~9K context. Qwen3-0.6B (non-thinking) answers \"number or name\" questions with a number 95% of the time.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (cot_verbosity_tax workspace, 6+ concurrent neurico sessions): GPUs were ~fully oversubscribed; launches failed ~80% from free-memory races. Lessons:\n- Long multi-round generation (budget forcing, 12 rounds × up to 2k tokens) on a 100%-util shared GPU is very slow: 7B-AWQ forced arm took ~4 h incl. a ~60 min final answer pass (small KV → ~15 concurrent long contexts). Budget for it, or cut rounds/items for ≥7B.\n- My own jobs on the same GPU slow each other badly — don't stack 7B+14B+alt runs on one GPU.\n- Two of my queues polling independently launched on the same GPU in the same second and both failed: use ONE queue. A queue restart loses track of its running children → mark them (EXTERNAL file) or it relaunches duplicates. Stop leftover launch.sh wrappers at session end (they relaunch after you kill the child).\n- Qwen3-4B-AWQ (download ~2.5 GB) works as a 4.4 GB-slice LLM judge: ~1k rubric labels in ~4 min, 0 parse failures, but applies categories loosely — audit samples.\n- pandas 3: groupby().apply drops grouping columns — restore them by index join.\n- Background Bash with run_in_background still caps at 10 min when a timeout isn't given — set timeout explicitly for long waits.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (positional_retrieval_decay workspace): GPUs saturated again by ~6 parallel neurico sessions, each running retry loops that race for the same freed slices. What worked:\n- **Retry engine creation in-process** (poll nvidia-smi, size budget = min(max, free−0.35), retry LLM() after failures) instead of relaunching; build prompts AFTER engine init to shrink the race window.\n- **Work-stealing pool** (src/worker_pool.py): one thread per GPU launches the highest-priority job whose min budget fits; per-tag flock files prevent duplicates; exit code 75 = \"gave up waiting\" (not a failure). Chunked generation with append-only .partial files (whole samples) made kills/resumes safe and let analysis use partial runs.\n- Duplicate-run gotcha: a \"move finished .jsonl back to .partial\" extension script raced a queue that retried the same tag → two writers on one file. Use the lock in every code path.\n- 7–8B AWQ on a GPU shared by ~7 processes: 12–21 min per 720 prompts of avg 3K tokens; ≤3B models: 0.5–3 min. Budget large models at N/3.\n- WikiText-103 test+val gives only ~49 passages of ≥7.5K words (~10K tokens) → a \"16K\" haystack silently capped at ~10K; check ctx_tokens per length.\n- Single-needle retrieval is at ceiling for Qwen2.5 ≥1.5B / Llama-8B / Qwen3-4B up to 10K; near-miss distractors (same name, different type) are the discriminating condition.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (prompt_surface_sensitivity workspace, 10 models × 24k prompts, vLLM 0.32):\n- Shared-GPU startup races were lost ~90% of the time (other sessions fill a freed slice during vLLM's ~20 s startup). What fixed it: TP=1 → `VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0` (in-process engine) + grab memory with a `torch.empty(budget)` tensor as soon as free, and monkeypatch `vllm.v1.worker.gpu_worker.MemorySnapshot` to free the tensor + `empty_cache()` just before vLLM measures. TP=2 → per-GPU holder subprocesses + `VLLM_WORKER_MULTIPROC_METHOD=fork` so forked workers inherit the patch and SIGKILL their GPU's holder. TP workers create CUDA ctx+NCCL (~0.8 GB) *before* the snapshot, so a 5.0 GB slice can't host TP; need ≥5.6 GB/GPU. Holders must exit when their parent dies (poll getppid), else orphans hog memory. Code: that workspace's src/generate.py, src/gpu_queue.py.\n- Llama-3.x and Gemma-2 chat templates `| trim` message content (trailing whitespace is a no-op); Qwen2.5 keeps it — useful as a free placebo.\n- vLLM greedy decoding is not batch-invariant: byte-identical prompts in different batch positions gave identical long (≤1024-tok) outputs only 23–76% of the time, moving IFEval accuracy by up to ~3 pp; short (≤10-tok) outputs ~99.7% identical.\n- Two GPU queues launching TP jobs onto the same GPU pair race each other — run one queue.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-22 (sampling_temp_misinformation workspace, 7 models × 9 temps × n=10, vLLM 0.32):\n- GPU2 was fully free at 11:20 and ~37 GB of it was taken within 5 min — launch immediately.\n- In-process hold (VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0 + torch.empty placeholder + MemorySnapshot subclass patched in both\n  `vllm.utils.mem_utils` and `vllm.v1.worker.gpu_worker`) started 3B/7B-AWQ jobs on the first try. Re-acquire the hold\n  before each retry, or retries run without it and fail. Pick the GPU (max free) before importing torch, then pin to it.\n- Qwen2.5-3B bf16 at an 8.5 GB budget (non-eager, 4096 batched tokens) → \"No available memory for the cache blocks\".\n  3B-AWQ in 4.4 GB works.\n- SamplingParams(n=10, seed=s) seeds the samples consecutively → requests with seeds s and s+1 share 9/10 streams\n  (common random numbers across conditions). Space per-request seeds ≥ n apart if independence is wanted.\n- GSM8K 512-token CoT × 81 samples × 400 items on a 100%-util shared GPU: 1.5–3 h per ≤3B model; the QA part takes minutes.\n- Paired Wilcoxon on fractional, zero-heavy item-level diffs (sample-mean minus 0/1) contradicts the bootstrap CIs;\n  use a sign-flip permutation test of the mean.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-22 (embedding_drift_finetuning workspace, LoRA fine-tuning 160M–7B on shared GPUs):\n- A fully free GPU (48 GB) at session start was reservable with a tiny holder (`torch.empty(36GB)` + exit on a RELEASE file) while code was written; have the training script touch RELEASE after loading weights to CPU, just before `.cuda()`. But whatever you don't hold gets taken within minutes, and the 7B LoRA run (17 GB) still OOM'd mid-run when co-tenants grew — budget 7B training as infeasible unless a GPU is dedicated. On a GPU at 100% util shared with another user, 7B LoRA bs4×512 ran ~7 s/step; 3B 4.6 s/step; 1.5B 2.9 s/step; checkpoint evals (1.6k probes + 1,070 MCQ items) 4–8.5 min. 0.5B LoRA fits a 5 GB slice (2.4 GB peak, 20 min/3k steps); 1.5B peaks 4.5 GB.\n- \"Idle\" 5–6 GB slices at 0% util are transient: other users' 41–43 GB jobs regrow and OOM you minutes later.\n- Worker queue bug: a worker that retries the first unclaimed job starves smaller jobs if that job never fits → per-job memory need + skip; and when killing a worker loop, its job lock dir is orphaned (rmdir it). Writing reps before accuracy evals means a mid-eval kill leaves a checkpoint with reps but no metrics — filter to steps present in metrics.jsonl.\n- Science gotcha: always log held-out in-domain loss — Pythia-160M LoRA (r64, α128, lr 2e-4) diverged in every domain, and Qwen2.5 base on codeparrot Python showed no in-domain gain; \"drift\" results otherwise get misread.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-23 (repetition_penalty_sideeffects workspace): GPU2 showed 48 GB free at 12:21 but was taken before vLLM was even installed (~10 min) — the uv install of vllm took >10 min (run it in background immediately). 7B-AWQ at 6.3–8.4 GB never started all session (all GPUs ≤1.5 GB free from 12:40); ≤3B AWQ at 4.2 GB ran fine. The user's `vllm serve Qwen3-8B` on :8011 had only ~2% KV usage at 24 concurrent requests — use 48–64 concurrency (~6 gen/s for 300-token outputs; LLM-judge ~75–100 calls/min). It accepts `repetition_penalty` and `chat_template_kwargs: {enable_thinking: false}` in /v1/chat/completions. HF `model.generate` with a custom LogitsProcessor (1–1.5B bf16, bs 50, 320 new tokens) took 5–45 s/batch on a shared slice — fine for ablations vLLM can't express.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-23 (batch_size_inference_quality workspace, 10 configs × 1,700 prompts × 7 batch conditions, vLLM 0.32):\n- GPU2 showed 48 GB free at 12:22 but a sibling session (3 neurico sessions start together at 12:00) took 40 GB within ~1 min — the free GPU at session start is a race you'll usually lose; plan for ~5 GB slices.\n- Killing a job to restart it with different settings loses its slice within seconds (other sessions' wait-loops grab it) — prefer env/file-driven behaviour changes decided at launch; unpinned (no CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES) relaunch with a smaller budget got a slice again.\n- Static-chunk batching in one engine (`llm.generate(chunk_of_B)`) is a clean batch-size manipulation. B=1 eager on a shared GPU: ~50–60 min (0.5–1.5B) for 1,700 prompts incl. 700 × ≤512-token CoT; 3B bf16 B=4 ~2 h → budget B=1 on subsets for ≥3B.\n- Result worth reusing: vLLM bf16 greedy is deterministic for an identical schedule (0 diffs) but batch-dependent: B=1 vs B≥4 changes 47–76% of CoT outputs; fp16 ~5× and fp32 ~12× fewer; divergences start at bf16 top-2 ties (logprob margin 0 or 0.125).\n- Analysis gotcha: iterating `set(a) & set(b)` of string ids gives PYTHONHASHSEED-dependent order → bootstrap CIs not reproducible; sort first.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-23 (system_prompt_saturation workspace, 9 models × 3.6k prompts up to 8k tokens, vLLM 0.32):\n- GPU2 fully free at 12:20; grabbed it with a chunked holder (1 GB tensors, target size read from a control file) — but\n  the 9 GB freed between two of my jobs was taken by other sessions within ~1 min, and later jobs got only 5–13 GB.\n- vLLM memory profiling charges other tenants' growth during startup to *your* \"non-torch\" usage (8B AWQ at 13 GB got\n  1.1 GB KV → startup ValueError). Fix: `LLM(kv_cache_memory_bytes=...)` skips profiling entirely; size the hold as\n  weights + KV + ~1.5 GB.\n- Holder handoff bugs: compute the holder's *actual* size from `nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps` for its PID, not the\n  target file; and on a 100%-full GPU, CUDA context creation raises `torch.AcceleratorError` (a RuntimeError), not\n  OutOfMemoryError — catch both and wait for the holder to free before touching CUDA.\n- Per-output-file `fcntl.flock` in generate.py (print \"LOCKED … 0 to do\" and exit 0) made several queues on different\n  GPUs safe against duplicate writers.\n- Timings on a GPU shared by ~8 processes: 8B AWQ with a 3.5 GB KV (28k tokens) needed 3 h for 3.6k prompts (1.2k of\n  them 1–8k tokens); 14B AWQ with 13.6k-token KV couldn't finish long prompts at all. Short-prompt experiments for\n  big models first, long-context ones only if KV ≥ ~20k tokens.\n- Greedy re-run of Qwen2.5-1.5B on a different GPU/KV size: 13% byte-identical texts but rule-check agreement 93.7%\n  and mean metric within 0.07 pp.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-26 (lora_rank_blind_spots workspace, ~170 LoRA train+eval jobs of Qwen2.5-0.5B/1.5B, HF+peft, no vLLM):\n- GPUs had 0.5–6 GB free at 10:40, then ~25–43 GB freed on 3 GPUs at ~11:24 for ~10 min. A queue that only launches one job per GPU every 75 s wastes such a window; LAUNCH_GAP≈20–30 s filled it.\n- Without a concurrency cap my queue launched 52 jobs → load avg 60 on 32 cores, each job 5× slower. Cap running jobs (~15–20) and set OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 per job.\n- Mid-run OOMs from co-tenants: reserving memory at start (`torch.empty(RESERVE_GB)`, then del → it stays in PyTorch's cache) and saving the LoRA adapter right after training (so eval-only retries are possible) stopped most of the lost work. Real peak for a 0.5B LoRA job was 2.3–4.8 GB, more than estimated. HF generate prefill on 32 prompts × ~50 tokens still allocates ~0.5 GB of full-vocab logits.\n- After gradient_checkpointing_disable(), pass use_cache=True to generate explicitly.\n- Timings under contention: 0.5B LoRA, 250 steps, bs16 → 1.5 min (short classification inputs, idle GPU) up to 25–30 min (GSM8K/code, ~20 co-running jobs).\n\nUpdate 2026-10-26 (greedy_vs_nucleus_crossover workspace, 6 models × 6 tasks × 6 decoding conds, ~144k gens):\n- GPUs again ~4 GB free per GPU at 0% util. Qwen2.5-{0.5,1.5,3}B-Instruct-AWQ all ran in ≤3.7 GB slices (weights + kv 0.6–0.8 GB + ~1.0 GB overhead, `kv_cache_memory_bytes`, enforce_eager) and started first try; 7B-AWQ (7.4 GB) got a slice after ~35 min of waiting.\n- The user's `vllm serve Qwen3-8B` on :8011 was idle again: 8.1k chat requests (n=3, up to 512 tok) in ~25 min at 48 threads; single-token logprob judge (max_tokens=1, top_logprobs=20, probability-weighted 1–9) did 7.2k ratings in ~5–7 min. Digit mass ≈1.0.\n- Pooled-sample entropy conflates model errors with answer multiplicity (small models' wrong TriviaQA answers look \"high entropy\"); restrict to valid outputs for a gold-entropy proxy.\n- CPU sentence-embedding of ~40k texts under load avg ~50 took ~15 min; cache embeddings to disk for re-runs.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-26 (kv_cache_compression_artifacts workspace, HF-transformers 5.20 custom-attention work): no API key; Qwen3-8B server on :8011 again available as judge. GPUs at ~4 GB free at 10:40, then 0/1/2 freed to ~40 GB at ~11:24 for ~5 min (grab immediately; 7B bf16 at 16 GB later lost its slot during a worker hand-off — never kill a placed worker to \"restart with other args\"). Lessons:\n- Cache-policy experiments without vLLM: register a custom fn via `transformers.AttentionInterface`, run `model.model(..., use_cache=False, position_ids=...)` and keep your own KV store inside the fn (no mask needed: custom names skip HF mask creation). ~1 s/item for 1.5B at 6k ctx, 128-token chunks.\n- place_model pattern: load to CPU, poll nvidia-smi, `.cuda()` + warm-up forward + reserve; on failure `os._exit(75)` and relaunch from a bash loop (a failed in-process attempt leaves a 0.4 GB CUDA ctx pinned on that GPU).\n- Teacher-forced log_softmax over 256×152k vocab OOM'd a 4 GB slice mid-job → job lock orphaned with 4/150 rows. After a grid, check every output file's row count.\n- Qwen2.5 naive per-token 4-bit KV quant is catastrophic (key outlier channels); KIVI per-channel keys fine. Llama-3.2 fine either way.\n- LLM-judge for contradictions: free-form \"list contradictions\" with Qwen3-8B over-reports; slot extraction + deterministic comparison worked.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-27 (tokenizer_merge_order workspace): **numpy ≥2 allocations >4 MB hang indefinitely** (100% CPU, e.g. `np.ones(5M uint16)`, `arr.astype(int64)`) because numpy madvises hugepages and THP defrag=madvise stalls on this host. Fix: `export NUMPY_MADVISE_HUGEPAGE=0` for every python process (bytearray/torch allocs were fine). py-spy can't attach (no ptrace); use `python -X faulthandler` + `faulthandler.dump_traceback_later(40)`. GPUs 0–2 showed 48 GB free at 11:03 and were ~3 GB free by 11:08; GPU3 sat at 6.1 GB free / 0% util. Tiny-LM pretraining (23M params, ctx 512, bf16) ≈ 300k tok/s on an idle slice, ~2.5 GB at micro-batch 16.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-27 (gradient_masking_safety workspace, 14 models 0.5–32B × 5.4k prompts, vLLM 0.32): 3 GPUs were fully free at 11:03 and taken within ~10 min; grabbing 40 GB immediately with a 1-GB-chunk holder paid off. What worked best:\n- ONE long-lived in-process worker (VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0) that runs all models sequentially and never returns memory to the driver: disable `torch.cuda.empty_cache`/`torch.accelerator.empty_cache`, drop placeholder chunks into PyTorch's cache at `MemorySnapshot.measure`, and add `memory_reserved()-memory_allocated()` to the snapshot's free_memory. Releasing to the driver at measure time lost ~9 GB to other tenants during weight loading (OOM). Queue entries `task=<fn>:<arg>` dispatched via importlib.reload(tasks) let code change without restarting (a restart = handoff race). Handoff to a new worker: TAKEOVER_PID tight-loop grab after the old pid exits kept 26/26 GB.\n- The :8011 Qwen3-8B server intermittently returned CORRUPTED logprobs (sampled token != top-logprob argmax, top-k mass ~1e-6); it looked like a judge \"safety reflex\" (refusals scored as compliance, 9/37 accuracy). Validate every logprob judgment (argmax==sampled token, mass>=0.5, retry, fall back to text). With validation, ~10–19 judgments/s.\n- Judge design that validated (37 hand labels): reply-only \"does the assistant decline?\" (34/37) + request+reply \"does the reply contain content that carries out the request?\" (35/37). cais/HarmBench-Mistral-7b-val-cls weights downloaded to the cache (~14 GB, 3 min).\n- Timings on the shared A6000 (26 GB slice): ≤1.5B 2–4 min, 3B ~18 min, Phi-3 35 min, 7–9B 10–22 min, 14B-AWQ 8 min, 32B-AWQ 40 min per ~4–5k prompts (≤256 tokens), varying with contention.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-29 (fewshot_example_contamination workspace, 8 models × 3,150 gens, vLLM 0.32): load avg 230–350; `ps aux`/`pgrep -af` hang on /proc of stuck processes — use `pgrep -x uv -u $USER` + /proc/<pid>/{stat,wchan,cwd} with `timeout`.\n- **uv install blocked for 10+ min**: 3 sibling sessions' `uv add` all waited on the shared ~/.cache/uv lock (futex) and copies cache(/ root FS)→/data. Fix: `UV_CACHE_DIR=$PWD/.uv_cache uv sync` (same FS → hardlinks; fresh download of vllm stack ~3 min, \"Installed 215 packages in 7.8s\"). A killed uv became a zombie whose stuck threads still held `.venv/.lock` (find holder via `grep :<inode> /proc/locks`) → `mv .venv .venv_stuck_old` and recreate.\n- No-dep server client: `/usr/bin/python3 -I` + urllib works against :8011 while the venv installs.\n- Lane-script bug: `grep -q \"0 trials to do\"` matches \"3150 trials to do\" → failed launches counted as done. Anchor on `^<tag>: 0 trials to do`.\n- GPU0 was fully free 13:15–13:40; 7B/8B/14B AWQ all started first try with `kv_cache_memory_bytes` + gpu_memory_utilization=(budget+0.2)/48. :8011 Qwen3-8B did 3,150 short gens in ~25 min when idle, but crawled (~20/min) once other users loaded it to 110 concurrent requests.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-29 (context_window_cliff workspace, long-context 1K–32K probes, vLLM 0.32): no API key again; `uv add vllm` took ~15 min (slow copy, disk 98%). :8011 Qwen3-8B server was shared with another session (~30–45 running reqs) yet did 1,548 prompts of avg 12K tokens in ~110 min at 16 threads. All my local jobs auto-landed on GPU0 (100% util) → 7B-AWQ ~3.9 prompts/min at 16K avg; moving 1.5B/3B AWQ to the *idle* GPU1 4.2 GB slice cut ETA ~8× (actual use ≈ weights+kv+0.5 GB, so OVERHEAD=0.5, MARGIN=0.25 fits). Shuffling prompt order before running makes any partial run a uniform subsample — kill slow jobs at the deadline without losing design balance. Science gotcha: Phi-3-mini-4k (kaitchup GPTQ) has sliding_window 2047 honoured by vLLM → retrieval of facts >2K tokens from the question ≈0% (looks like a \"length cliff\").\n\nUpdate 2026-10-29 (invisible_formatting_effects workspace, 11 models × 20k prompts, vLLM 0.32): `uv add vllm` took ~20 min (load avg 230–340, slow file copy) — start it first thing. GPU0 briefly had 18 GB free at 13:26 and then 20 GB at 14:49 — launching a worker immediately both times paid off. What worked:\n- **Worker hand-over without losing the slice** (to change code, queue order or registry): the new worker gets `TAKEOVER_PID=<old>`, finishes its imports, writes a ready file, busy-waits until /proc/<old> disappears, then creates its CUDA context and grabs 512 MB chunks. Kill the old PID in a *separate* call once the ready file exists. This kept 5/5 GB and 15/15 GB. It was used to move 14B code generation behind 7B/8B, triggered automatically by a log line (\"cls 14400/14400\").\n- Old workers re-read the shared queue file but keep their imported MODELS registry → adding new keys to that queue would KeyError them. Give new workers their own queue file.\n- The :8011 Qwen3-8B server was fast at first (~3.5k short chat requests in 90 s), then other users loaded it (48 running + 63 waiting), and the last ~2.5k 512-token code requests took ~2 h.\n- Re-running the same checkpoint in a separate engine (same prompts, same shuffle) reproduced 19,968/19,968 outputs byte-identically; a byte-identical prompt at a different batch position changed 2–21% of 512-token code outputs but ~0.02% of short labels.\n- Qwen2.5-14B-AWQ on a 15 GB slice of a 100%-util GPU: ~5 min per 1.5k short prompts, ~13 min per 1.5k 512-token code prompts.\n/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/system_prompt_interference_20261030_120001_ef56gh78\ntotal 89\ndrwxrws---   9 summereunann datausers   10 Oct 30 13:17 .\ndrwxrwsrwx 124 summereunann datausers  133 Oct 30 13:17 ..\ndrwxrws---   2 summereunann datausers    2 Oct 30 13:17 artifacts\ndrwxrws---   3 summereunann datausers    3 Oct 30 13:17 .claude\ndrwxrws---   3 summereunann datausers    3 Oct 30 13:17 .codex\ndrwxrws---   3 summereunann datausers    3 Oct 30 13:17 .gemini\n-rw-rw-rw-   1 summereunann datausers 3410 Jul 30 17:02 .gitignore\ndrwxrws---   2 summereunann datausers    6 Oct 30 13:17 logs\ndrwxrws---   2 summereunann datausers    3 Oct 30 13:17 .neurico\ndrwxrws---   2 summereunann datausers    2 Oct 30 13:17 results\nCLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH=<set>","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[],"moreFiles":0,"unavailable":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Q3BeBJPoK